
======================================================================================================
THE OFF-CIRCULAR ACTION FOR THE dS-UNRUH MODIFIED-INERTIA FRAMEWORK
======================================================================================================
  a0 = c H_Lambda / Z,  Z = sqrt(32 pi / 3) = 5.78881  ->  a0 = 9.3600e-11 m/s^2 (canonical)
  equivalently a0 = (c/2) sqrt(G rho_Lambda): EXACTLY HALF the free-fall acceleration at
  the dark-energy density. kappa = 1/2 is this framework's own coefficient and is not in
  the prior literature; note also that 32pi/3 is the Einstein-coupling conversion factor
  and CANCELS in that reduction, so the content is the ONE number kappa, which is FITTED,
  not derived (the kappa-forcing door was closed 2026-06-17).
  Alternate footing carried throughout: a0 = 1.1300e-10 m/s^2.

======================================================================================================
S1. f from mu by quadrature, the circular identity, and closed forms for BOTH kernels
======================================================================================================
  DEFINITION (this is the only input):   f(u) = u^-2 * Integral_0^u v mu(v) dv
  so that  u^2 f(u) = Lambda(u) = Integral_0^u v mu(v) dv,  hence  Lambda'(u) = u mu(u).
  The circular identity is then an IDENTITY of the quadrature, not an extra assumption:
      (1/u) d/du [u^2 f(u)] = Lambda'(u)/u = mu(u).

  alpha=1 (retired)
    mu(u)  = (sqrt(4*u**2 + 1) - 1)/(2*u)
    f(u)   = (2*u*sqrt(4*u**2 + 1) - 4*u + asinh(2*u))/(8*u**2)
  [OK  ] alpha=1 (retired): (1/u) d/du[u^2 f] returns mu EXACTLY (sympy residual 0) -- the quadrature and the circular identity are the same statement
    limits: f(u->oo) = 1/2 (want 1/2, i.e. exact Newtonian kinetic term);  f/u -> 1/3 as u->0 (want 1/3)
  [OK  ] alpha=1 (retired): f interpolates 1/2 <-> u/3, so the action's Newtonian limit is EXACTLY (1/2) m |xdot|^2 with no residual correction

  alpha=2 (in force)
    mu(u)  = u/sqrt(u**2 + 1)
    f(u)   = (u*sqrt(u**2 + 1) - asinh(u))/(2*u**2)
  [OK  ] alpha=2 (in force): (1/u) d/du[u^2 f] returns mu EXACTLY (sympy residual 0) -- the quadrature and the circular identity are the same statement
    limits: f(u->oo) = 1/2 (want 1/2, i.e. exact Newtonian kinetic term);  f/u -> 1/3 as u->0 (want 1/3)
  [OK  ] alpha=2 (in force): f interpolates 1/2 <-> u/3, so the action's Newtonian limit is EXACTLY (1/2) m |xdot|^2 with no residual correction
  [OK  ] alpha=1 f reproduces the corpus's committed closed form in mi_three_corrections_priorart_2026.py EXACTLY (independent re-derivation agrees)
  [OK  ] *** NEW *** alpha=2 generating function in closed form: f_2(u) = [u sqrt(1+u^2) - asinh(u)] / (2 u^2)   (the corpus carried only f_1)

======================================================================================================
S2. *** THE OFF-CIRCULAR ACTION *** and its honest fourth-order Euler-Lagrange equation
======================================================================================================
  THE ACTION.  With f the generating function of S1 and phi the Newtonian potential of the
  BARYONS ONLY (no dark matter anywhere in this framework):

      S[x] = Integral dt  m ( |xdot|^2 f( |xddot| / a0 )  -  phi(x) )

  This is local, Galilei-invariant, rotation-invariant, and reduces to the exact Newtonian
  action (1/2) m |xdot|^2 - m phi in the limit a0 -> 0 because f(u->oo) = 1/2 (S1).
  It is the UNRESTRICTED object the corpus never wrote: previous work varied only inside the
  two-parameter circular family, which cannot establish that the real EL equation holds.

  ITS EULER-LAGRANGE EQUATION.  L depends on xddot, so the equation is FOURTH order:

      d^2/dt^2 [ (|xdot|^2 / a0) f'(u) ahat ]  -  2 f(u) xddot  -  2 (d/dt f(u)) xdot
                                                                          = grad phi
      with  u = |xddot| / a0,  ahat = xddot / |xddot|.

  Every term is written out; nothing is dropped. The first term is the higher-derivative
  one, the third vanishes iff u is constant along the trajectory.

  RESULT, for GENERIC f (F0=f(u0), F1=f'(u0), F2=f''(u0), F3=f'''(u0) all kept free,
  so nothing about the kernel is assumed):
    solving EL = 0 on the circular path gives   g_bar = GM/R^2 = Omega**2*R*(2*F0*a0 + F1*Omega**2*R)/a0
    the closure demands                          g_bar = A mu(A/a0) = Omega**2*R*(2*F0*a0 + F1*Omega**2*R)/a0
  [OK  ] *** A CIRCULAR ORBIT SOLVES THE FULL FOURTH-ORDER EL EQUATION EXACTLY, and the equation it satisfies is precisely the framework's closure g_bar = A mu(A/a0) with mu = 2f + u f' *** (sympy residual 0). This PROMOTES the corpus's restricted-family variation to the real equation of motion, for ANY kernel
  [OK  ] f'' and f''' are ABSENT from the circular result -- the higher derivatives of f, which are exactly what makes the equation fourth-order and carries the Ostrogradsky modes (S5), drop out identically on a circle. This is WHY circles are the family that works
  [OK  ] the tangential projection of EL vanishes identically on the circle, so the solution is consistent (no secular torque; angular momentum conserved)
  [OK  ] alpha=1: with the explicit kernel substituted, the circular EL solution is g_bar = A mu_1(A/a0) EXACTLY (residual 0)
  [OK  ] alpha=2: with the explicit kernel substituted, the circular EL solution is g_bar = A mu_2(A/a0) EXACTLY (residual 0)

  NEGATIVE CONTROLS (these MUST fail, or the check above is vacuous):
  [OK  ] control 'f = mu/2 (the naive guess)' does NOT reproduce the closure (residual nonzero as required)
  [OK  ] control 'f = Lambda/u^3 (right quadrature, wrong power)' does NOT reproduce the closure (residual nonzero as required)

======================================================================================================
S3. A SECOND exactly-solvable family: the interpolating function is TRAJECTORY-DEPENDENT
======================================================================================================
  Uniform straight-line acceleration: x(t) = (a t^2 / 2, 0), so u is constant and ahat is
  constant, but |xdot|^2 = a^2 t^2 GROWS. Feeding this into the same EL equation:

      d^2/dt^2[(a^2 t^2 / a0) f'(u)] - 2 f(u) a  =  dphi/dx
      => force = -dphi/dx = 2 a [ f(u) - u f'(u) ]  ==  a mu_lin(u),
         mu_lin(u) = 2[f(u) - u f'(u)]      versus circular   mu(u) = 2 f(u) + u f'(u).

  CAVEAT, stated because it matters: this family is unbounded (v -> oo), so the v^2
  prefactor grows without bound and the action is not finite over an infinite time. The EL
  equation is still exact pointwise, which is all that is being read off here.

  alpha=1
    mu_lin(u)               = (2*u*sqrt(4*u**2 + 1) - 8*u + 3*asinh(2*u))/(4*u**2)
    mu_lin -> 1 as u->oo   (Newtonian limit must be 1 for BOTH families)
    deep-regime series: mu_lin = 4*u**3/5
                        mu_circ= -u**3 + u
  [OK  ] alpha=1: the straight-line family has the correct Newtonian limit mu_lin -> 1, so the trajectory-dependence is a DEEP-REGIME effect only
    deep-regime leading power: mu_lin ~ u^3,  mu_circ ~ u^1
  [OK  ] alpha=1: deep regime goes as u^3 on a straight line but u^1 on a circle -- the same action gives DIFFERENT interpolating functions on different trajectory shapes
      at u=0.100:  mu_lin= 7.7779e-04  mu_circ= 9.9020e-02  ratio=     127.3x
      at u=0.030:  mu_lin= 2.1545e-05  mu_circ= 2.9973e-02  ratio=    1391.2x
      at u=0.010:  mu_lin= 7.9977e-07  mu_circ= 9.9990e-03  ratio=   12502.3x

  alpha=2
    mu_lin(u)               = u/sqrt(u**2 + 1) + 3/(u*sqrt(u**2 + 1)) - 3*asinh(u)/u**2
    mu_lin -> 1 as u->oo   (Newtonian limit must be 1 for BOTH families)
    deep-regime series: mu_lin = 2*u**3/5
                        mu_circ= -u**3/2 + u
  [OK  ] alpha=2: the straight-line family has the correct Newtonian limit mu_lin -> 1, so the trajectory-dependence is a DEEP-REGIME effect only
    deep-regime leading power: mu_lin ~ u^3,  mu_circ ~ u^1
  [OK  ] alpha=2: deep regime goes as u^3 on a straight line but u^1 on a circle -- the same action gives DIFFERENT interpolating functions on different trajectory shapes
      at u=0.100:  mu_lin= 3.9576e-04  mu_circ= 9.9504e-02  ratio=     251.4x
      at u=0.030:  mu_lin= 1.0790e-05  mu_circ= 2.9987e-02  ratio=    2779.2x
      at u=0.010:  mu_lin= 3.9996e-07  mu_circ= 9.9995e-03  ratio=   25001.4x

  READ: this is NOT a defect peculiar to this framework -- Milgrom (1994; 2022 PRD
  106:064060) showed that modified-inertia theories generically have orbit-dependent
  interpolating functions, and that the algebraic relation holds only for single-frequency
  trajectories. This is that statement, made concrete for THIS action and THIS kernel.

======================================================================================================
S4. THE QUANTITATIVE OFF-CIRCULAR RESIDUAL -- how far apart are the closure and the action?
======================================================================================================
  METHOD, and it is exact rather than an integration: the framework's closure
  xddot = -a(r) rhat with a(r) from mu(a/a0) a = g_bar is a genuine CENTRAL force, so its
  orbits have exact energy and angular-momentum integrals. Pick a pericentre/apocentre pair
  -> (E, L) -> the exact state (r, v_r, v_theta) at every radius, with NO ODE error. Then
  build the action's fourth-order EL residual as a function of that state (all time
  derivatives eliminated using the closure itself) and evaluate it around the orbit.
  Reported as |EL residual| / |grad phi|: 0 means the action and the closure agree.

